-- A "What I learned" section per project, and a tool list rebuilt from what the projects actually use.
alter table public.projects add column lessons text not null default '' check (length(lessons) <= 5000);

update public.projects p set lessons = l.lessons
from jsonb_to_recordset('[{"slug":"casfyre","lessons":"Write the decision down before the code. Payments especially: when to charge, when to refund, what actually counts as agreeing to something. I keep short decision records now, and they stop me from re-arguing the same thing a month later.\nHydration errors are usually just invalid HTML. One crash came down to a link inside another link in the mobile header. The browser quietly fixed it before React loaded, so the two trees didn''t match.\nNot everything needs end-to-end encryption. Work messages have to be searchable, reportable and reviewable in a dispute, so I went with strict server-side access rules and left a clean spot to add encryption later where it makes sense.\nMoving hosting is its own project. Going from Vercel to Cloudflare Workers meant rethinking cron jobs, secrets and caching, not just changing the deploy command."},{"slug":"kanto","lessons":"Building fast leaves holes. After about five weeks I ran a full audit on my own code and it found a stored XSS in search results and tables without row-level security. Both got fixed and tested, but I''d rather catch that stuff earlier now.\nSecurity is a lot of small, boring decisions. Script nonces per request, origin checks, what staff can see, how long data is kept. Writing them down in one security model is what stopped me from re-deciding them every week.\nAnonymous posting is harder than it sounds. People still need to delete their own posts and mods still need to moderate them, without anything linking a post back to an account in the open.\nDocs drift. One of the audit findings was simply that the docs said one thing and the code did another, so now docs get checked like code."},{"slug":"vaikusruum","lessons":"A client''s small change is usually a system problem. When some text boxes wouldn''t take new lines, the real fix was one shared text system for every block on the site, not patching each box.\nThe editor matters more than the website. The person running the site uses the admin way more than I do, so most of my time went into making editing obvious: click a text, change it, done.\nPlan before touching a live client site. Start with a read-only look at what''s there, write down what done means, and then change things in small steps.\nGood SEO doesn''t need tricks. Proper titles, descriptions and structured data got the site showing up in searches without making a single fake page."},{"slug":"the-last-customer","lessons":"Finishing is a skill on its own. Getting from works on my machine to an actual Steam release (builds, store page, trailer, review) taught me more than any tutorial did.\nPlan how systems talk to each other early. I kept adding mechanics as I went and they got tangled, which is most of why the game feels rough. Now I think about structure before I start building.\nAtmosphere carries a horror game. The lighting, the VHS look and the pacing did more than any single mechanic."},{"slug":"casmine","lessons":"Pin your tools. Casmine runs on one specific custom Godot build with Voxel Tools, locked by version and checksum, so it builds the same way every time.\nMeasure before guessing. I keep benchmark worlds and a capture tool, so I can compare frames and frame times before and after a change instead of going by feel.\nUse a real reference. Comparing my captures next to actual Xbox 360 screenshots shows what''s off much faster than looking at my own game alone."}]'::jsonb) as l(slug text, lessons text)
where p.slug = l.slug;
update public.projects set body = '## The game

You work night shifts at a gas station. Your friend goes missing, and finding him takes you through liminal spaces, puzzles, and things that are pretending to be customers. It came out on Steam in October 2024.

## Honestly

It was my first big project, and I worked on it for about half a year. It''s rough in a lot of places and anything I''d make now would be way better. But that''s kind of the point. It''s where I actually learned game development: enemies, puzzles, choices, menus, lighting, and getting a whole game out the door. Pretty much every mistake I made on it taught me something I still use.' where slug = 'the-last-customer';

